WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.816
Embedded Rust often interacts directly
with hardware through memory-mapped

2
00:00:03.896 --> 00:00:06.860
registers, peripheral access crates,
hardware abstraction layers,

3
00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:08.246
and device-specific drivers.

4
00:00:08.326 --> 00:00:11.692
These layers are where language-level
safety meets hardware behavior that the

5
00:00:11.772 --> 00:00:13.279
compiler cannot fully understand.

6
00:00:13.359 --> 00:00:15.670
Memory-mapped I/O is different from
ordinary memory.

7
00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.764
Register reads and writes can have side
effects, timing requirements,

8
00:00:18.844 --> 00:00:21.958
ordering constraints, or relationships
with clocks, pins, interrupts,

9
00:00:22.038 --> 00:00:23.345
DMA engines, and power states.

10
00:00:23.425 --> 00:00:27.042
The defensive goal is to represent those
constraints through types and APIs wherever

11
00:00:27.122 --> 00:00:27.624
practical.

12
00:00:27.704 --> 00:00:29.965
Some hardware access requires `unsafe`
internally.

13
00:00:30.045 --> 00:00:32.808
That does not make the code wrong, but it
changes the review model.

14
00:00:32.888 --> 00:00:35.651
Unsafe code should be small, justified,
documented, and tested.

15
00:00:35.731 --> 00:00:39.197
A safety comment should explain the
hardware and memory assumptions that make the

16
00:00:39.277 --> 00:00:41.437
operation valid, not simply assert that it
is safe.

17
00:00:41.517 --> 00:00:43.527
Peripheral ownership and exclusivity
matter.

18
00:00:43.607 --> 00:00:47.123
If two parts of firmware believe they own
the same peripheral or pin configuration,

19
00:00:47.203 --> 00:00:48.761
behavior can become unpredictable.

20
00:00:48.841 --> 00:00:51.955
Initialization order, clock setup, pin
mode, interrupt configuration,

21
00:00:52.035 --> 00:00:55.351
and reset behavior should be expressed in
a way that safe callers cannot misuse

22
00:00:55.431 --> 00:00:56.084
accidentally.

23
00:00:56.164 --> 00:00:58.425
The best hardware abstractions protect
invariants.

24
00:00:58.505 --> 00:01:01.117
Safe APIs should guide callers toward
valid register states,

25
00:01:01.197 --> 00:01:04.563
valid transitions, bounded operations, and
documented timing expectations.

26
00:01:04.643 --> 00:01:07.858
Reviewers should pay close attention to
places where safe code crosses into

27
00:01:07.938 --> 00:01:10.500
low-level assumptions that cannot be
checked by Rust alone.
